<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jarrow</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jarrow is a town on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a> in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Its monastery of St Paul, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-biscop">Benedict Biscop</a> in the early 680s and joined with Wearmouth, was a centre of Northumbrian learning where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> lived and wrote, and the dedication stone of its church, dated 685, survives. In modern times Jarrow was a shipbuilding town, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow-march">Jarrow March</a> of 1936 against unemployment became a symbol of the Depression.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jarrow.jpg" alt="Jarrow" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xaphire at Dutch Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jarrow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
